--TEST--
Core\Crypto::deriveKey, ::expandKey and ::agree answer a key that carries `secret` with it — each reaches an `array<secret bytes>`, a `secret bytes` parameter and a `secret` property with no reveal on the path, the agreement chain runs end to end on the qualifier alone, and a width costs one written reveal — `rule:security/secret-qualifier`, `rule:core-classes/crypto-interop-tier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// tests/conformance/reject/crypto-a-derived-key-is-a-secret-bytes-like-a-drawn-one.nvst
// asserts the direction no running program can reach: narrowing one of these
// answers into a plain `bytes` does not compile. This is the other direction,
// and it is the one a program lives in — a qualified answer is accepted
// wherever a `secret bytes` is written, so a key a derivation or an agreement
// produced travels to the member that uses it with nothing written on the way.
// That is what the octets alone cannot show: the same 32 octets answered as a
// plain `bytes` behave identically here and differ only in where they may go.

final class Vault {
    public function constructor(public string $label, public secret bytes $key) {}

    // The two shapes a program writes around a key it was handed: a parameter
    // that takes one, and a property that holds it for as long as the object
    // lives. Each of the three answers arrives at both of them directly.
    public static function sealFor(secret bytes $key, bytes $message): bytes {
        return Core\Crypto::seal($message, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305);
    }
}

bytes $salt = Core\Bytes::fill(16, 65);
bytes $message = "attack at dawn" as bytes;

Core\Crypto\KeyPair $mine = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::X25519);
Core\Crypto\KeyPair $theirs = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::X25519);

// One PBKDF2 output, one HKDF output and one shared secret, each straight from
// the member that answered it into a collection whose element type is the
// qualified one.
array<secret bytes> $keys = [
    Core\Crypto::deriveKey("correct horse battery staple", $salt, 100000),
    Core\Crypto::expandKey(Core\Crypto::generateKey(), $salt, "encryption"),
    Core\Crypto::agree($mine, $theirs->publicKey()),
];
array<string> $from = ["derived", "expanded", "agreed"];

int $round = 0;
foreach (Core\Arr::range(0, 2) as int $i) {
    Vault $held = new Vault($from[$i], $keys[$i]);
    bytes $sealed = Vault::sealFor($keys[$i], $message);
    if (Core\Crypto::open($sealed, $held->key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305) == $message) {
        $round += 1;
    }
}
echo "round trips ", $round, " of 3", "\n";

// `agree` answers the material `expandKey` takes, and the chain between them
// is qualified at every step: neither the shared secret nor the key drawn out
// of it is ever a plain value. Both ends run it, and a message sealed at one
// opens at the other, so the key the qualifier travelled with is one key.
secret bytes $mineSide = Core\Crypto::expandKey(
    Core\Crypto::agree($mine, $theirs->publicKey()),
    $salt,
    "chat"
);
secret bytes $theirsSide = Core\Crypto::expandKey(
    Core\Crypto::agree($theirs, $mine->publicKey()),
    $salt,
    "chat"
);
int $chain = 0;
if ($mineSide == $theirsSide) {
    $chain += 1;
}
bytes $toThem = Vault::sealFor($mineSide, $message);
if (Core\Crypto::open($toThem, $theirsSide, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305) == $message) {
    $chain += 1;
}
echo "the agreed chain holds ", $chain, " of 2", "\n";

// A width is the one question these keys will not answer on their own:
// `Core\Bytes::length` has no `secret bytes` parameter, so measuring one is a
// written `Core\Secret::revealBytes` with its reason beside it. What comes back
// through that door is the same key — the plain copy opens what the qualified
// original sealed — which is the claim in full: the qualifier rides with the
// answer and is charged for at the one place it comes off.
int $wide = 0;
int $sameKey = 0;
foreach (Core\Arr::range(0, 2) as int $i) {
    bytes $plain = Core\Secret::revealBytes($keys[$i], "a conformance case measures the key it was handed");
    if (Core\Bytes::length($plain) == 32) {
        $wide += 1;
    }
    if (Core\Crypto::open(Vault::sealFor($keys[$i], $message), $plain, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305)
        == $message) {
        $sameKey += 1;
    }
}
echo "32 octets ", $wide, " of 3", "\n";
echo "revealed copies open ", $sameKey, " of 3", "\n";
--EXPECT--
round trips 3 of 3
the agreed chain holds 2 of 2
32 octets 3 of 3
revealed copies open 3 of 3
